quick take last week we saw a historic  moment in sports where players from  almost  every major league in the u.s sat out  from games in protest of the police  shooting of jacob lake  to talk more about this and the wnba's  role in fighting for justice i'm joined  by  la sparks guard chelsea gray chelsea  thanks so much for joining me  thank you for having me chelsea so can  you bring me back to last week  what were the emotions like when you  guys all decided not to play  so we had gotten on the bus we weren't  the first game  um of that day so if the first game  didn't play it's not like we were going  to play in the second game  one thing that we stressed going into  this was that  you know it's solidarity whatever we  were going to do we were going to do it  all together and we got to the arena  washington and atlanta were already  discussing play or not play and we  wanted everybody's voice to be heard  and we made the decision not to play and  stand in solidarity  solidarity with the milwaukee bucks in  their decision not to play  um and i think it was an important one  especially to do it all together yeah  and like you said  all together you know this they started  the nba in the wnba  and you know you guys are never shy to  talk about these things and take a  stance  but then it trickled down into all these  other leagues across  sports what did it feel like to see that  and to have that unity from not just  basketball players but to have it  throughout the whole world of sports  yeah it was important um to be able to  see  all these different leagues and  different games you know really stand in  solidarity and understand you know  you know we hoop that's our profession  we play sports that's a profession but  we're human and it's a  human human issue going on humanity  issue going on right now  um it's a lot bigger than ourselves and  stepping outside of that and really  having those tough conversations  is was extremely important that's what  we were able to do on those days  and when you guys made a decision to not  play you still  had people make statements on the court  and have these conversations on the  court what kind of impact do you think  that has  other than just maybe giving out a tweet  before or after  it's important to be able to you know  for our youth to look up and look at the  tv and you see somebody that looks like  them our president  um the wnba president um neko glumakay  also my teammate  made a great statement understanding  that we're going to do it together and  it's her job to you know kind of voice  that  out and really have everybody on the  same page which we were  um but it's important to see those types  of people  you know black and brown women are used  to being you know  left behind and to be able to see ariel  atkins up there standing up saying  her piece neko gumma k elizabeth  williams up there stating  um different things it's it's it's vital  um to be able to have that and have  those open dialogues and actually say  how we feel  how we feel as a and you just said it  that  black and brown women are used to being  left behind and right now you're wearing  a shirt say  say her name um in stance with brianna  taylor can you talk more about  that and how you feel as a black woman  trying to stand up for other black women  in the world  um you know i think my teammate said it  best candice she was just like we're  you know with the wba we're majority of  a minority in the world world  right there's a lot of black and brown  women that's playing in this league but  you go outside of it and we're  definitely a minority we're black and  we're women  and then you add in that i am part of  the lgbtq plus community it's important  um that's a whole minority right there  um  even us standing there saying we're  we're doing that it's part of the  movement  it's it's part of the social justice  it's part of the change  and um you know we're used to you know  kind of being left behind  so we didn't care that we were standing  up saying what we believed  saying how we felt and um we have player  moms in here and we're doing that for  that little kid  so when they grow up they don't have to  deal with things like this  and you just started recently the  chelsea gray assist for equality program  can you tell me more about that and  why you felt the need to start it  i'm just trying to do my part and making  sure people are educated and understand  the importance of voting  so i'll partner with both equality  california and  rock the vote i'm donating fifty dollars  for every assist i dish out twenty five  dollars to equality california  and twenty five dollars to rock the vote  and um  the sparks have decided to match which  i'm excited about and i'm hopefully  going to get other organizations to  either match or donate  whatever the case may be but i wanted to  kind of  be in a relationship or have a  relationship with these companies  because it's important to educate people  on the importance of voting not even  at the presidential level right at the  local level the state level to  understand these different policies and  who they're voting for to push these  policies through  um so equality california is from  an organization that really tries to  push and help people of the lgbtq plus  community  and then rock the vote was with the  hooper's initiative to get these  athletes out there  to you know explain understand and  educate others of the importance of  voting at all levels  and why did you feel like personally  that you wanted to  go out there and talk about voting and  put your name on it  and tell them yeah just talking with my  peers being involved and educating  myself  outside of these circles has really  helped me to understand  and i knew you're you know growing up  you're supposed to vote for the  president  uh but beyond that it always seemed  complicated and difficult  um and now i'm i'm trying to be part of  that  you know that movement to be able to  educate people you know it's not it's  not that hard it's not that difficult  register to vote go somewhere to vote  sending your ballot whatever the case  may be according to your state  um but it's important every vote counts  absolutely every vote does count and  what do you think that it means to have  somebody like you and  other big name athletes like lebron  james and stuff have these um  movements and how impactful do you think  that can be  i think growing up you want to see on  you know on tv  in radios on now we got instagram and  twitter and things like that  um to be able to see somebody that looks  like them  that's not just an athlete but it's  going out there and you know trying to  strive for change  um using using your platform and  you know everybody has some sort of  platform  no matter how big or small but what you  do with it  is important you have people looking  that's that's going to be okay i  remember hearing this i remember hearing  that  um so now when you get in those times  that you can vote you pull somebody else  along and that's what we're trying to do  right  now and in the wmba you guys have  always been really at the forefront of  these issues and  using your platform like you said what  have you guys had more discussions about  what else else you're going to do to  take  steps forward and the next thing you can  do  to bring light to these issues i think  just  pushing the same initiatives that we've  been talking about  um actually having conversation with  people that  you know have had you know we had a  conversation with brianna taylor's  mother  and the importance of that you really  felt i like just have  just hearing her words it ate me up  and everybody has a cousin everybody has  an uncle  you know a relative that could have been  in their position  and so um i think right now we're in  discussion of you know how do we  how do we do something now and have a  plan of action that can really strive to  make change  and pushing people to vote the  importance of voting  um just keep pushing the initiative that  we're talking about and completing the  senses  and understanding the importance of that  and what  are the next steps that you would like  to see us as a country take  forward that proves that we're making  strides  in you know uh in towards racial  equality  and what is justice  um i would say just educating yourself  on the voting system  how it works what to win what to do to  ensure your vote counts  um why it's important you know actually  researching candidates  i know that i need to do more research  myself  and it's okay to do that it's okay to  educate yourself to make sure you're  making  the decision that you feel is right and  your vote is a way of having a voice in  this country  and um i just challenge people to do  that to go out and understand educate  themselves and help the next person  and um i think that's the way we strive  to make change  and uh just just uh have a have an  opportunity to be open to dialogue  and um have those uncomfortable  conversations  and that is like something that a lot of  people may not  be comfortable doing you know having  those uncomfortable conversations how do  people  start having those kinds of  conversations with maybe people who  aren't trying to listen  um you know that's a difficult question  but i would just say  you know find someone that is opening up  a little bit you all you need is a  sliver  you know you just need a little bit you  know you ask one question then it bleeds  to other things  um but challenge yourself um  if you come across somebody you know  relating to that person  you know open them up to you know see a  different point of view but that comes  with educating yourself  first and that's what i had to learn how  can i have these powerful  and conversations to be able to explain  my point of view without educating  myself  and i'm still doing that it's a forever  ending process  because you always want to get better  for the next person you learn something  you want to share with somebody else  um so educating yourself i feel like is  a huge thing  and uh keep planting those seeds for  future generations  absolutely chelsea thank you so much for  joining me  and for all you're doing for the fight  for equality thank you so much  thank you all for joining us and for  joining us in this conversation

